    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve for \\(\\sin x\\) given that \\(\\sec x + \\tan x = \\frac{4}{3}\\), we start by expressing \\(\\sec x\\) and \\(\\tan x\\) in terms of \\(\\sin x\\) and \\(\\cos x\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sec x = \\frac{1}{\\cos x}, \\quad \\tan x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\n\\]\n\nThus, the given equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\cos x} + \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{4}{3}\n\\]\n\nWe can combine the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1 + \\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{4}{3}\n\\]\n\nCross-multiplying to clear the fraction gives:\n\n\\[\n1 + \\sin x = \\frac{4}{3} \\cos x\n\\]\n\nRecall the Pythagorean identity:\n\n\\[\n\\cos^2 x + \\sin^2 x = 1\n\\]\n\nWe need to express \\(\\cos x\\) in terms of \\(\\sin x\\) or vice versa. First, isolate \\(\\cos x\\) on one side of the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\cos x = \\frac{3(1 + \\sin x)}{4}\n\\]\n\nSubstitute this expression for \\(\\cos x\\) into the Pythagorean identity:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{3(1 + \\sin x)}{4} \\right)^2 + \\sin^2 x = 1\n\\]\n\nSimplify the left-hand side:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{9(1 + \\sin x)^2}{16} + \\sin^2 x = 1\n\\]\n\nMultiply through by 16 to clear the denominator:\n\n\\[\n9(1 + \\sin x)^2 + 16\\sin^2 x = 16\n\\]\n\nExpand the square term:\n\n\\[\n9(1 + 2\\sin x + \\sin^2 x) + 16\\sin^2 x = 16\n\\]\n\nDistribute the 9:\n\n\\[\n9 + 18\\sin x + 9\\sin^2 x + 16\\sin^2 x = 16\n\\]\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n\\[\n9 + 18\\sin x + 25\\sin^2 x = 16\n\\]\n\nRearrange to form a quadratic equation in terms of \\(\\sin x\\):\n\n\\[\n25\\sin^2 x + 18\\sin x + 9 - 16 = 0\n\\]\n\nSimplify further:\n\n\\[\n25\\sin^2 x + 18\\sin x + 3 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\( \\sin x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 25 \\), \\( b = 18 \\), and \\( c = 3 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\sin x = \\frac{-18 \\pm \\sqrt{18^2 - 4 \\cdot 25 \\cdot 3}}{2 \\cdot 25}\n\\]\n\nCalculate the discriminant:\n\n\\[\n18^2 - 4 \\cdot 25 \\cdot 3 = 324 - 300 = 24\n\\]\n\nSo,\n\n\\[\n\\sin x = \\frac{-18 \\pm \\sqrt{24}}{50}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the square root of 24:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{24} = 2\\sqrt{6}\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\sin x = \\frac{-18 \\pm 2\\sqrt{6}}{50} = \\frac{-9 \\pm \\sqrt{6}}{25}\n\\]\n\nThe two possible values for \\(\\sin x\\) are:\n\n\\[\n\\sin x = \\frac{-9 + \\sqrt{6}}{25} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\sin x = \\frac{-9 - \\sqrt{6}}{25}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sin x\\) must be between -1 and 1, we check which value falls within this range:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{6} \\approx 2.449, \\quad \\sqrt{6} - 9 \\approx -6.551\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(\\frac{-9 - \\sqrt{6}}{25} \\approx -0.875\\) is negative and less than -1, which is not valid. Therefore, the only valid solution is:\n\n\\[\n\\sin x = \\frac{-9 + \\sqrt{6}}{25}\n\\]\n\nHence, the only possible value of \\(\\sin x\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{-9 + \\sqrt{6}}{25}}\n\\]",
