      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We have the equation $\\sec x + \\tan x = \\frac{4}{3}.$ To divide this into two separate equations, we use a common denominator on the left side, which gives us\n\\[\\frac{\\sec x + \\tan x}{1} = \\frac{\\sec x}{\\tan x + 1}.\\]\nCross multiplying, we get\n\\[\\sec x = \\frac{4}{3}(\\tan x + 1),\\]\nwhich we can solve for $\\sec x$ and $\\tan x$ separately.\n\nSince $\\sec x=\\frac{1}{\\cos x}$, we have $\\cos x = \\frac{3}{4}.$\nSince\n\\begin{align**}\n\\cos x &= \\frac{3}{4}, \\\\\n\\sqrt{1-\\cos^2 x} &= \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{16}} = \\boxed{\\frac{5}{4}},\n\\end{align**}\nwe have\n\\begin{align**}\n\\sin^2 x &= 1 - \\cos^2 x, \\\\\n\\sin^2 x &= 1 - \\frac{9}{16}, \\\\\n\\sin^2 x &= \\boxed{\\frac{7}{16}}.\n\\end{align**}\n\nWe then solve $\\sin^2 x = \\frac{7}{16}$ for \n\\[\\sin x = \\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{16}}.\\]\nThe answer is \\boxed{\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{16}}}",
